<body>
<h1>Unit 11: Quantity of Heat</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Heat &amp; Thermodynamics</p><p class="summary">Quantity of heat is where the first law becomes arithmetic: energy accounting with a heat capacity, a latent heat and a clear statement of which system you are describing.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>Q = m c dT requires no phase change, a constant specific heat over the range, and a clearly defined system.</li><li>Specific heat capacity is per unit mass; the molar heat capacity is C = M c, where M is the molar mass.</li><li>During a phase change the temperature stays constant while heat is added, because the energy goes into changing the structure rather than the motion of the particles.</li><li>Latent heat enters as Q = m L; the specific latent heat of vaporisation of water at 100 degrees C is about 2.26e6 J/kg.</li><li>Internal energy is a state function of the body; heat is the transfer, and the first law writes dQ = dU + dW with work done by the system positive.</li><li>Calorimetry rests on the statement that heat lost by the hot body equals heat gained by the cold one - true only for an isolated system.</li><li>Heat capacity is quoted along constant volume or constant pressure because only the path-independent change of internal energy is the same for both; the difference between them is the work done by expansion.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>The constant-c approximation fails for gases, whose heat capacity varies with temperature, and for any interval that crosses a phase change.</li><li>Latent heat is not a universal constant: it varies with pressure and temperature, and for water it falls as the critical point is approached.</li><li>Calorimetry assumes an isolated system; in a real cup the heat capacity of the container must be included, and it often dominates the error.</li><li>Specific heat of water is commonly approximated as 4.18 kJ/(kg.K) over 0 to 100 degrees C, but it varies by more than 1 % across that range.</li><li>Water's large specific heat is a symptom of hydrogen-bonded network rearrangements, not a separate slow-to-heat mechanism, and it is the reason oceans moderate coastal climate.</li></ul><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Specific heat of water</td><td>about 4.18 kJ/(kg.K)</td></tr><tr><td>Latent heat of vaporisation of water (100 degrees C)</td><td>about 2.26e6 J/kg</td></tr><tr><td>Specific latent heat of fusion of water at 0 degrees C</td><td>about 3.34e5 J/kg</td></tr><tr><td>Energy to raise 1 kg of water by 1 K</td><td>about 4.18 kJ</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>How is energy distributed among the translational, rotational and vibrational modes of a molecule as its temperature rises?</b></p><p class="status">Under active scrutiny</p><p>Equipartition gives the high-temperature limit and quantum theory gives the low-temperature limit; the crossover in real materials is modelled, not derived.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Can the heat capacity of a complex solid be computed from first principles to experimental accuracy?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>Modern ab initio methods approximate it well for simple crystals; no exact general theory exists.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Plan a calorimetry experiment to determine the specific heat of a metal and list every systematic error you can foresee.</li><li>Show quantitatively why 1 kg of steam at 100 degrees C can release more energy than 1 kg of water at 100 degrees C.</li><li>Estimate the energy needed to heat a classroom by 10 degrees C and compare it with a student's daily food intake.</li></ol><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/index">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 2</a></li><li><a href="https://physics.nist.gov/cuu/Uncertainty/basic.html">NIST: Measurement uncertainty</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-4-heat-transfer-specific-heat-and-calorimetry">OpenStax: Heat Transfer, Specific Heat and Calorimetry</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-5-phase-changes">OpenStax: Phase Changes</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
